00:02:21.540 --> 00:02:23.190
RUSSELL WOLF: Cloud
Source Repositories

00:02:23.190 --> 00:02:26.850
is a generally
available service on GCP

00:02:26.850 --> 00:02:31.050
that allows you to host
private Git repositories.

00:02:31.050 --> 00:02:33.900
Because those repositories
are hosted on GCP,

00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:36.600
it makes it really easy to
integrate with other GCP

00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:40.260
services, such as
Stackdriver Debugger, Cloud

00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:43.290
Build, and Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:02:43.290 --> 00:02:46.380
There are three areas where
Cloud Source Repositories

00:02:46.380 --> 00:02:48.030
really excels.

00:02:48.030 --> 00:02:51.600
First, is with its
high scalability.

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:55.170
Google has invested in a highly
scalable Git infrastructure

00:02:55.170 --> 00:02:58.740
for many years to support the
needs of two of the largest Git

00:02:58.740 --> 00:03:00.780
projects that exist--

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:03.390
Chromium and Android.

00:03:03.390 --> 00:03:07.500
Cloud Source Repositories uses
that same highly scalable Git

00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:09.330
infrastructure,
which means that you

00:03:09.330 --> 00:03:14.610
can host large files, large
folders, large repositories,

00:03:14.610 --> 00:03:19.680
and do it all for many users
with a very low latency.

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:23.490
Next, Cloud Source Repositories
has high reliability,

00:03:23.490 --> 00:03:26.610
because all repositories are
hosted in multiple data centers

00:03:26.610 --> 00:03:30.440
across different regions.

00:03:30.440 --> 00:03:33.380
In theory, even if half
of the world's network

00:03:33.380 --> 00:03:35.480
was disconnected,
all repositories

00:03:35.480 --> 00:03:37.790
would still be
able to be served.

00:03:37.790 --> 00:03:40.970
We even isolate data centers
and perform periodic disaster

00:03:40.970 --> 00:03:45.530
recovery tests, just to be
sure your source code is safe.

00:03:45.530 --> 00:03:48.650
And finally, to help meet
your organization's compliance

00:03:48.650 --> 00:03:51.770
needs, Cloud Source
Repositories doesn't just

00:03:51.770 --> 00:03:54.660
integrate with identity
and access management,

00:03:54.660 --> 00:03:57.230
meaning that you get to use
the same identity you use

00:03:57.230 --> 00:03:59.480
for the rest of
your organization,

00:03:59.480 --> 00:04:02.690
and use a consistent
permission model used

00:04:02.690 --> 00:04:05.670
with the rest of
your cloud resources.

00:04:05.670 --> 00:04:09.470
It also features audit logging,
which means that if something

00:04:09.470 --> 00:04:12.710
were to go wrong, such
as codex filtration,

00:04:12.710 --> 00:04:15.200
you can be sure that you
can do an investigation

00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:18.380
and figure out what
happened, who did it,

00:04:18.380 --> 00:04:21.339
and when it happened.

00:04:21.339 --> 00:04:23.260
Cloud Source
Repositories also does

00:04:23.260 --> 00:04:26.480
daily globally
redundant backups.

00:04:26.480 --> 00:04:29.060
Which means that if some
disaster were to occur,

00:04:29.060 --> 00:04:31.060
your source code is safe.

00:04:31.060 --> 00:04:34.780
And finally, with
automatic encryption

00:04:34.780 --> 00:04:38.200
in transit with per
repository encryption keys,

00:04:38.200 --> 00:04:40.600
your IP is protected.

