00:06:22.650 --> 00:06:25.020
NIKHIL KAUL: The other
goal of us providing

00:06:25.020 --> 00:06:27.420
Cloud Source
Repositories is making

00:06:27.420 --> 00:06:29.970
developers more productive.

00:06:29.970 --> 00:06:31.800
And traditionally,
what we see is

00:06:31.800 --> 00:06:34.440
developers spend a lot
of time trying to find

00:06:34.440 --> 00:06:37.260
answers each and every day--

00:06:37.260 --> 00:06:41.910
answers to questions like how
a function should be called,

00:06:41.910 --> 00:06:44.520
what does the class look
like, where does the class

00:06:44.520 --> 00:06:48.330
get instantiated, why
is something failing,

00:06:48.330 --> 00:06:50.400
or who changed
the code resulting

00:06:50.400 --> 00:06:52.680
into the code base failing?

00:06:52.680 --> 00:06:56.490
And answers to those
questions can be easily found

00:06:56.490 --> 00:06:59.475
through code search
functionality of Cloud Source

00:06:59.475 --> 00:07:01.270
Repositories.

00:07:01.270 --> 00:07:05.410
Code search allows you to search
across your entire repository

00:07:05.410 --> 00:07:08.210
with a single query.

00:07:08.210 --> 00:07:10.160
This is especially
useful when you

00:07:10.160 --> 00:07:12.860
don't have all of your
organization's repositories

00:07:12.860 --> 00:07:16.140
cloned down from the server
to your local machine,

00:07:16.140 --> 00:07:18.140
or all of your local
repos aren't up to date

00:07:18.140 --> 00:07:20.060
with the server.

00:07:20.060 --> 00:07:21.470
Indexing is super fast.

00:07:21.470 --> 00:07:24.200
So even if you're in a large
organization with commits

00:07:24.200 --> 00:07:26.780
constantly coming
in, you're always

00:07:26.780 --> 00:07:29.340
searching against
up-to-date code.

00:07:29.340 --> 00:07:31.520
In fact, Cloud
Source Repositories

00:07:31.520 --> 00:07:35.570
uses the same document indexing
and retrieval mechanisms

00:07:35.570 --> 00:07:37.820
used by Google
search, which means

00:07:37.820 --> 00:07:41.210
that it can handle your
searches quickly and across very

00:07:41.210 --> 00:07:44.410
large corpuses of code.

00:07:44.410 --> 00:07:47.140
There are three areas where
code search for Cloud Source

00:07:47.140 --> 00:07:49.930
Repositories really excels.

00:07:49.930 --> 00:07:53.440
First, with support for
regular expression search.

00:07:53.440 --> 00:07:55.930
That means you can craft
queries to answer even the most

00:07:55.930 --> 00:07:57.860
complex of questions.

00:07:57.860 --> 00:08:01.420
Second, by having a
syntax-aware index.

00:08:01.420 --> 00:08:03.730
That means that you're not
just searching for text,

00:08:03.730 --> 00:08:06.550
you can also search for
specific entity types,

00:08:06.550 --> 00:08:09.860
such as a class or a function.

00:08:09.860 --> 00:08:13.420
And finally, with results
suggestions as you type,

00:08:13.420 --> 00:08:14.920
you can get answers
to the questions

00:08:14.920 --> 00:08:17.018
you're looking for right
away, without having

00:08:17.018 --> 00:08:18.685
to wade through any
search result pages.

00:08:23.030 --> 00:08:25.040
Cloud Source
Repositories also helps

00:08:25.040 --> 00:08:27.530
you keep your code secure.

00:08:27.530 --> 00:08:29.930
It's generally not a good
idea to check service account

00:08:29.930 --> 00:08:34.580
credentials or security keys
into a version control system.

00:08:34.580 --> 00:08:38.130
However, sometimes you
may accidentally do that.

00:08:38.130 --> 00:08:41.419
And that's where Cloud Source
Repositories is there to help.

00:08:41.419 --> 00:08:43.429
With the Pushblock
feature enabled,

00:08:43.429 --> 00:08:47.660
if you try to push a commit to
the server that contains a GCP

00:08:47.660 --> 00:08:52.300
service account credential
or a security key,

00:08:52.300 --> 00:08:53.980
that push will be blocked.

00:08:53.980 --> 00:08:56.320
And you'll be given
information telling you

00:08:56.320 --> 00:09:00.940
what file was in violation
and why it was blocked.

00:09:00.940 --> 00:09:03.490
If your commit was just fine,
it makes it to the server.

