And earlier today,
I was actually

00:13:38.890 --> 00:13:41.140
testing out deploying
my Cloud Function

00:13:41.140 --> 00:13:45.130
from a third-party service
to build a nice integration.

00:13:45.130 --> 00:13:48.040
And because I was doing that,
I downloaded some GCP service

00:13:48.040 --> 00:13:50.680
account credentials
to my machine.

00:13:50.680 --> 00:13:54.110
Those are contained
in this key.json file.

00:13:54.110 --> 00:13:56.320
Now later in the day,
I may go and make

00:13:56.320 --> 00:13:59.410
an innocuous change,
such as fixing

00:13:59.410 --> 00:14:04.530
this typo from this
repository to this repository.

00:14:04.530 --> 00:14:07.590
And I'll track it with Git.

00:14:07.590 --> 00:14:10.300
Make a quick comment
saying, fixing typo.

00:14:14.740 --> 00:14:17.470
And push it to the server.

00:14:17.470 --> 00:14:19.170
Now, you see I made
a mistake there.

00:14:19.170 --> 00:14:20.460
I was going quick.

00:14:20.460 --> 00:14:23.340
I just did a gitadd dot
that [? edited ?] everything

00:14:23.340 --> 00:14:27.390
in the repository that wasn't
being tracked to be tracked.

00:14:27.390 --> 00:14:31.890
And here we can see that
my push was rejected.

00:14:31.890 --> 00:14:34.020
Pushblock has said
that the push has

00:14:34.020 --> 00:14:36.882
been rejected because we detect
that it contains a private key.

00:14:36.882 --> 00:14:38.340
Please check the
following commands

00:14:38.340 --> 00:14:40.620
and confirm it's intentional.

00:14:40.620 --> 00:14:43.480
I can go ahead and execute
this gitshow command,

00:14:43.480 --> 00:14:46.230
and it will show the contents
of the private key, which

00:14:46.230 --> 00:14:48.600
I'm not going to do on stage.

00:14:48.600 --> 00:14:51.625
And if I detect
there was actually

00:14:51.625 --> 00:14:53.500
something in there that
wasn't a private key,

00:14:53.500 --> 00:14:55.860
and this was a
false detection, I

00:14:55.860 --> 00:14:59.430
can go ahead and just
run gitpush and override,

00:14:59.430 --> 00:15:02.260
to say I don't want a key check.

00:15:02.260 --> 00:15:05.200
And with that, we've seen how
Pushblock and Cloud Source

00:15:05.200 --> 00:15:06.700
Repositories can
help you [? from ?]

00:15:06.700 --> 00:15:08.320
making those critical
mistakes that

00:15:08.320 --> 00:15:10.230
can really put
your organization's

00:15:10.230 --> 00:15:12.780
security at risk.

00:15:12.780 --> 00:15:15.375
And with that, let's go ahead
and switch back to the slides.

00:15:15.375 --> 00:15:17.250
And Nikhil can talk to
us about the next part

00:15:17.250 --> 00:15:19.722
of our presentation,
which is deploying.

00:15:19.722 --> 00:15:21.930
NIKHIL KAUL: So with that,
we finished the first part

00:15:21.930 --> 00:15:25.650
of the presentation, which was
essentially developing an app.

00:15:25.650 --> 00:15:28.590
When it comes to deploying
an app, many of us

00:15:28.590 --> 00:15:30.930
are deploying to
different environments.

00:15:30.930 --> 00:15:33.210
These could be VMs.

00:15:33.210 --> 00:15:35.550
These could be containers.

00:15:35.550 --> 00:15:37.880
These could be
serverless environments.

00:15:37.880 --> 00:15:40.540
Or these could be
mobile platforms.

00:15:40.540 --> 00:15:43.470
Irrespective of whether you're
using Google Compute Engine

00:15:43.470 --> 00:15:46.870
VMs, Google Kubernetes
Engine for deploying

00:15:46.870 --> 00:15:49.450
containers,
serverless frameworks,

00:15:49.450 --> 00:15:54.880
such as App Engine or
functions, or mobile platforms,

00:15:54.880 --> 00:15:59.590
like Firebase, Cloud Source
Repositories integration

00:15:59.590 --> 00:16:01.770
with Cloud Build can come
in really handy here.

00:16:05.970 --> 00:16:08.830
Cloud Build is Google's
continuous integration,

00:16:08.830 --> 00:16:11.370
continuous delivery platform.

00:16:11.370 --> 00:16:15.620
And Cloud Source Repositories
integrates with it.

00:16:15.620 --> 00:16:18.022
In fact, as you would
see within the demo,

00:16:18.022 --> 00:16:19.480
the good part about
the integration

00:16:19.480 --> 00:16:25.720
is that there are no CS servers
to set up, manage, or maintain.

00:16:25.720 --> 00:16:28.400
As you would see,
within a few clicks,

00:16:28.400 --> 00:16:31.540
you will be able to
achieve automation

00:16:31.540 --> 00:16:34.060
from source to production.

00:16:34.060 --> 00:16:36.700
And the good thing is
you can deploy your app

00:16:36.700 --> 00:16:38.750
to different environments.

00:16:38.750 --> 00:16:41.260
In fact, if you are
someone who likes

00:16:41.260 --> 00:16:45.370
to manage their build
configuration as code,

00:16:45.370 --> 00:16:48.490
you can use cloudbuild.yaml
specifically

00:16:48.490 --> 00:16:50.590
for specifying build steps.

00:16:50.590 --> 00:16:52.030
You can specify
the steps you want

00:16:52.030 --> 00:16:54.460
to perform as a part of build.

00:16:54.460 --> 00:16:55.900
You can specify
the steps you want

00:16:55.900 --> 00:16:57.940
to perform as a part of test.

00:16:57.940 --> 00:16:59.740
And you can specify
the steps you

00:16:59.740 --> 00:17:02.050
want to perform as a
part of deploy, as well.

00:17:04.819 --> 00:17:07.950
The workflow looks
something like this.

00:17:07.950 --> 00:17:10.430
As a developer,
I write the code,

00:17:10.430 --> 00:17:13.430
and I check it in Cloud
Source Repositories.

00:17:13.430 --> 00:17:16.280
From there on, Cloud
Build takes over.

00:17:16.280 --> 00:17:18.349
And Cloud Build takes
over [? mainly ?]

00:17:18.349 --> 00:17:21.050
because we have set
up a trigger to notify

00:17:21.050 --> 00:17:26.530
Cloud Build that a code with
a change has been checked in.

00:17:26.530 --> 00:17:29.875
Next, as Cloud Build
takes over, Cloud Build

00:17:29.875 --> 00:17:33.490
can build both Docker
artifacts, as well as

00:17:33.490 --> 00:17:36.160
non-container-specific
artifacts.

00:17:36.160 --> 00:17:40.920
Example of these can be
Gradle, Maven, Go, Bazel, et

00:17:40.920 --> 00:17:43.130
cetera, et cetera.

00:17:43.130 --> 00:17:45.290
Once the artifacts
have been created,

00:17:45.290 --> 00:17:48.170
Cloud Build can run
different types of tests,

00:17:48.170 --> 00:17:51.870
whether these are unit
tests or integration tests.

00:17:51.870 --> 00:17:54.500
If you're working with
containers specifically,

00:17:54.500 --> 00:17:57.050
in the Artifact
Management space, which

00:17:57.050 --> 00:18:01.160
is the fourth point
over there, Cloud Build

00:18:01.160 --> 00:18:02.960
can help you scan
those artifacts for

00:18:02.960 --> 00:18:04.430
[? vulnerabilities, ?] as well.

00:18:04.430 --> 00:18:06.380
We'll talk more about
it in the later parts

00:18:06.380 --> 00:18:08.010
of the presentation.

00:18:08.010 --> 00:18:12.990
And finally, if
everything looks great,

00:18:12.990 --> 00:18:16.760
you can deploy that app
to different environments

00:18:16.760 --> 00:18:18.620
in the deployment phase.

00:18:21.220 --> 00:18:24.510
However, when you're working
in a continuous integration

00:18:24.510 --> 00:18:27.530
and continuous
delivery environment,

00:18:27.530 --> 00:18:31.320
you need notifications as
a part of that process.

00:18:31.320 --> 00:18:34.880
You might want notifications
during the start, as well as

00:18:34.880 --> 00:18:36.200
the end.

00:18:36.200 --> 00:18:38.090
And that's where the
notification piece

00:18:38.090 --> 00:18:39.860
comes in handy.

00:18:39.860 --> 00:18:42.710
RUSSELL WOLF: Both the Cloud
Source Repositories and Cloud

00:18:42.710 --> 00:18:45.740
Build offer integrations
with Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:18:45.740 --> 00:18:47.720
That means that when a
new commit is checked

00:18:47.720 --> 00:18:51.620
into your repository, a
new bill is kicked off,

00:18:51.620 --> 00:18:55.880
or a build completes, you
can generate a Pub/Sub event

00:18:55.880 --> 00:18:58.210
to a Pub/Sub topic.

00:18:58.210 --> 00:19:00.550
You can then have a
Cloud Function configured

00:19:00.550 --> 00:19:03.520
to monitor that Pub/Sub
topic, watching out

00:19:03.520 --> 00:19:05.790
for these new events coming in.

00:19:05.790 --> 00:19:09.600
And when a new event comes in
and a Cloud Function triggers,

00:19:09.600 --> 00:19:11.850
you can have it invoke
a third-party service

00:19:11.850 --> 00:19:14.625
such as Slack,
Email, or any other

00:19:14.625 --> 00:19:15.750
that you want to work with.

00:19:19.450 --> 00:19:21.547
Before we go on to look
at a demo with this all,

00:19:21.547 --> 00:19:23.380
I'm going ask Nikhil
to go ahead and walk us

00:19:23.380 --> 00:19:25.960
through the workflow of what
we'll be doing in the demo.

00:19:25.960 --> 00:19:26.770
NIKHIL KAUL: Sure.

00:19:26.770 --> 00:19:28.870
So we've set up a
really simple demo

00:19:28.870 --> 00:19:31.060
to demonstrate a
continuous integration

00:19:31.060 --> 00:19:33.340
and continuous
delivery pipeline.

00:19:33.340 --> 00:19:35.350
That's the first
phase of the demo.

00:19:35.350 --> 00:19:37.090
And the second
phase of the demo is

00:19:37.090 --> 00:19:41.320
essentially targeted at getting
a notification to Slack.

00:19:41.320 --> 00:19:44.560
Let's break down
these two phases

00:19:44.560 --> 00:19:47.860
and go into more
detail, one by one.

00:19:47.860 --> 00:19:49.840
Both of these two
phases essentially

00:19:49.840 --> 00:19:53.740
start at the same place, which
is developer writing a code

00:19:53.740 --> 00:19:57.160
and checking it in into
Cloud Source Repositories.

00:19:57.160 --> 00:20:01.450
In the first phase, what happens
is as developer writes a code

00:20:01.450 --> 00:20:05.020
and checks it into Cloud Source
Repositories, a build gets

00:20:05.020 --> 00:20:07.000
triggered, because
Cloud Build gets

00:20:07.000 --> 00:20:10.510
a notification through the
build trigger which we have set.

00:20:10.510 --> 00:20:13.000
Once the build gets
triggered, Cloud Build

00:20:13.000 --> 00:20:17.470
essentially deploys the app to
the test version of App Engine

00:20:17.470 --> 00:20:19.810
and runs end-to-end tests.

00:20:19.810 --> 00:20:22.900
If the tests pass,
Cloud Build then

00:20:22.900 --> 00:20:25.230
deploys the app to
the production version

00:20:25.230 --> 00:20:26.860
of App Engine.

00:20:26.860 --> 00:20:30.160
That completes the first phase
of the continuous integration

00:20:30.160 --> 00:20:32.290
and continuous delivery process.

00:20:32.290 --> 00:20:35.010
In the second stage,
what we are doing is

00:20:35.010 --> 00:20:37.960
we are sending a
notification to Slack.

00:20:37.960 --> 00:20:41.080
The steps look
something like this.

00:20:41.080 --> 00:20:44.490
Starts again with developer
checking in the code.

00:20:44.490 --> 00:20:47.670
Cloud Source Repositories
publishes that event

00:20:47.670 --> 00:20:51.100
to Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:20:51.100 --> 00:20:56.380
Cloud Function then subscribes
to the Pub/Sub event.

00:20:56.380 --> 00:20:59.060
And since it's subscribing
to the Pub/Sub event,

00:20:59.060 --> 00:21:02.350
it posts the message which
it received in the event

00:21:02.350 --> 00:21:03.760
to the Slack.

00:21:03.760 --> 00:21:06.440
Let's see both of
these demos in action.

00:21:09.225 --> 00:21:11.100
RUSSELL WOLF: Let's
switch over to the demos.

00:21:16.630 --> 00:21:20.840
Here, we can see an application
already deployed to production.

00:21:20.840 --> 00:21:23.740
It's a very simple app.

00:21:23.740 --> 00:21:25.510
Basically, it's a guest book.

00:21:25.510 --> 00:21:29.920
You can write a message in
this box here, such as Welcome

00:21:29.920 --> 00:21:34.260
to Next 2019, sign
in the Guestbook,

00:21:34.260 --> 00:21:37.110
and it's posted there along
with the user who wrote it.

00:21:37.110 --> 00:21:39.510
You can switch over to
other Guestbooks, as well.

00:21:39.510 --> 00:21:42.060
And they'll have a distinct
set of messages too.

00:21:42.060 --> 00:21:46.100
And you can log-in and
log-out of this application.

00:21:46.100 --> 00:21:48.590
Now, the title App
Engine Guestbook

00:21:48.590 --> 00:21:50.450
maybe isn't the
most suitable one,

00:21:50.450 --> 00:21:52.610
so let's go ahead and
change it to Next 2019

00:21:52.610 --> 00:21:58.180
Guestbook, just something
easy for us to do here.

00:21:58.180 --> 00:22:00.760
And we'll change
it first by going

00:22:00.760 --> 00:22:02.110
to Cloud Source Repositories.

00:22:02.110 --> 00:22:05.220
And I have no idea, actually,
where this code is declared.

00:22:05.220 --> 00:22:07.580
But it's a simple code
change, so I can do it.

00:22:07.580 --> 00:22:11.050
So I'm going to search
for App Engine Guestbook.

00:22:11.050 --> 00:22:14.780
And it found the result
in one of these repos.

00:22:14.780 --> 00:22:18.810
And I see it's here, so I
can just click Edit Code.

00:22:18.810 --> 00:22:21.920
And this is now going to launch
me an instance of Cloud Shell.

00:22:21.920 --> 00:22:24.350
It's going to make sure
that this repository is

00:22:24.350 --> 00:22:26.060
mirrored into this VM.

00:22:26.060 --> 00:22:29.860
And I'll have this complete
workspace in the cloud.

00:22:29.860 --> 00:22:31.970
Here, I already have it
mirrored over, actually.

00:22:31.970 --> 00:22:33.770
So it's just asking me
to change directory.

00:22:33.770 --> 00:22:35.100
And I'm at that line of code.

00:22:35.100 --> 00:22:40.060
So I'm going to change it
from App Engine to Next 2019.

00:22:40.060 --> 00:22:43.147
We'll add this to Git.

00:22:43.147 --> 00:22:44.980
We'll [? git ?] status
and make sure I'm not

00:22:44.980 --> 00:22:49.780
checking in any security
keys, or anything like that.

00:22:49.780 --> 00:22:55.605
[? Git commit-- ?]
I'm changing title.

00:22:55.605 --> 00:22:57.480
And then finally, I'll
push it to the server.

00:23:01.070 --> 00:23:03.250
Now, as this commit gets
pushed to the server,

00:23:03.250 --> 00:23:06.560
just like Nikhil mentioned,
we're kicking off a build.

00:23:06.560 --> 00:23:09.460
So let's switch over
to Cloud Build now.

00:23:09.460 --> 00:23:15.650
And we can refresh and see that
a new build has been invoked.

00:23:15.650 --> 00:23:18.950
Clicking into there, we can
see that as three distinct

00:23:18.950 --> 00:23:21.330
build steps.

00:23:21.330 --> 00:23:24.720
We'll dig into those into
detail in one minute.

00:23:24.720 --> 00:23:27.180
Here, you can also see that
we have real time logs that

00:23:27.180 --> 00:23:28.950
are being produced
from this build,

00:23:28.950 --> 00:23:31.497
so we can see what's going on.

00:23:31.497 --> 00:23:33.080
Let's go ahead and
take a look at this

00:23:33.080 --> 00:23:34.770
build trigger that
we talked about.

00:23:34.770 --> 00:23:37.270
So when code made it to
Cloud Source Repositories,

00:23:37.270 --> 00:23:41.310
it kicked off this build
and told it what to do.

00:23:41.310 --> 00:23:44.660
First, we notice that
there's a trigger type.

00:23:44.660 --> 00:23:47.330
The trigger type allows
you to specify when you

00:23:47.330 --> 00:23:50.250
want this trigger to activate.

00:23:50.250 --> 00:23:54.200
You have the options
between a branch or a tag.

00:23:54.200 --> 00:23:56.360
And here, I've chosen branch.

00:23:56.360 --> 00:23:58.190
And I can choose a
regex that I want

00:23:58.190 --> 00:24:00.800
to match for when I want
that build to be invoked.

00:24:00.800 --> 00:24:02.440
In this case, I'm
doing a dot star

00:24:02.440 --> 00:24:03.980
because I only have one branch.

00:24:03.980 --> 00:24:05.620
And I just want to
trigger off that.

00:24:05.620 --> 00:24:07.730
But I could also specify
an exact branch name

00:24:07.730 --> 00:24:12.700
or regex for a set of branches
I want to kick off builds for.

00:24:12.700 --> 00:24:16.000
I can also choose to filter
those build by changed files.

00:24:16.000 --> 00:24:19.020
And so here, I could
use a regex to say,

00:24:19.020 --> 00:24:20.770
I only want to trigger
builds if there are

00:24:20.770 --> 00:24:22.450
changes in my source folder.

00:24:22.450 --> 00:24:24.040
Or I don't want
to trigger builds

00:24:24.040 --> 00:24:26.920
if it's just a change to a
config file or a markdown file.

00:24:29.650 --> 00:24:32.410
Once you've decided when you
were going to invoke a build,

00:24:32.410 --> 00:24:33.970
you then get to
decide what you want

00:24:33.970 --> 00:24:37.012
to do when a build is invoked.

00:24:37.012 --> 00:24:38.720
You do that inside of
Build Configuration

00:24:38.720 --> 00:24:41.000
by choosing between
using a Docker file,

00:24:41.000 --> 00:24:43.850
or a Cloud Build
configuration file.

00:24:43.850 --> 00:24:46.970
In this case, we've used a
Cloud Build configuration file,

00:24:46.970 --> 00:24:49.300
cloud.yaml.

00:24:49.300 --> 00:24:51.900
Let's take a look
at this cloud.yaml.

00:24:51.900 --> 00:24:54.150
We can jump back to Cloud
Source Repositories.

00:24:54.150 --> 00:24:57.125
I could search for it,
but it's right here.

00:24:57.125 --> 00:24:59.000
And so here, we can see
the first step that's

00:24:59.000 --> 00:25:00.560
declared in the cloud.yaml.

00:25:06.460 --> 00:25:09.100
This step says, we're going
to deploy the current code

00:25:09.100 --> 00:25:13.090
to a test version on App
Engine without promoting it.

00:25:13.090 --> 00:25:15.580
I'm just declaring
to use this existing

00:25:15.580 --> 00:25:19.960
image stored in Container
Registry for G Cloud.

00:25:19.960 --> 00:25:22.240
I'm saying to form the
[? app deploy ?] commands

00:25:22.240 --> 00:25:25.150
and specifying the
app.yaml for App Engine

00:25:25.150 --> 00:25:28.960
and an index.yaml for
the backing data store.

00:25:28.960 --> 00:25:32.650
And then I'm specifying these
two parameters version tests.

00:25:32.650 --> 00:25:34.420
So I'm naming that
version, and I'm

00:25:34.420 --> 00:25:37.860
saying not to promote
it to production.

00:25:37.860 --> 00:25:41.580
After that, if and only
if that step completes,

00:25:41.580 --> 00:25:43.080
I proceed to the
next step, which

00:25:43.080 --> 00:25:47.040
runs end-to-end tests against
the test version on App Engine.

00:25:47.040 --> 00:25:49.620
So here, I'm just
using another image

00:25:49.620 --> 00:25:52.230
that's already stored on
Container Registry, which

00:25:52.230 --> 00:25:53.790
is an Ubuntu image.

00:25:53.790 --> 00:25:57.090
I'm opening into Bash,
installing some prerequisites,

00:25:57.090 --> 00:26:02.060
and then executing this
runtests.bash script.

00:26:02.060 --> 00:26:05.000
Here, we can see I'm just
declaring the URL for my test

00:26:05.000 --> 00:26:07.160
environment on App Engine.

00:26:07.160 --> 00:26:11.850
And I'm executing
the end-to-end tests.

00:26:11.850 --> 00:26:14.240
If those tests all
succeed, I proceed

00:26:14.240 --> 00:26:17.300
to the next step, which
deploys the current code

00:26:17.300 --> 00:26:19.760
to a new version on App Engine.

00:26:19.760 --> 00:26:22.520
That's promoted to
serve production.

00:26:22.520 --> 00:26:25.970
Again, I just use
that G Cloud image

00:26:25.970 --> 00:26:28.420
and call that same app deploy.

00:26:28.420 --> 00:26:31.190
But this time, I'm not
naming it to a test version,

00:26:31.190 --> 00:26:34.450
and I am letting it
promote to production.

00:26:34.450 --> 00:26:37.770
That's really all there is for
setting up this automated test

00:26:37.770 --> 00:26:40.050
environment, running
my end-to-end tests,

00:26:40.050 --> 00:26:44.280
as well as promoting to
production if those succeed.

00:26:44.280 --> 00:26:46.830
I recognize many of you
might not use App Engine.

00:26:46.830 --> 00:26:50.190
There are many other
deployment run times.

00:26:50.190 --> 00:26:52.920
If you were to
deploy to Kubernetes,

00:26:52.920 --> 00:26:55.500
there's Cloud Build
steps for that.

00:26:55.500 --> 00:26:57.360
If you wanted to deploy
to Cloud Functions,

00:26:57.360 --> 00:27:00.630
it's as simple as specifying
your function name.

00:27:00.630 --> 00:27:02.460
And finally, there's
even steps if you're

00:27:02.460 --> 00:27:06.230
trying to go to mobile, and
you want to deploy to Firebase.

00:27:06.230 --> 00:27:08.240
Now that we've seen how
the cloudbuild.yaml does

00:27:08.240 --> 00:27:10.820
that final part of showing
what we should build,

00:27:10.820 --> 00:27:13.010
let's return to the build
and see what's happened.

00:27:15.630 --> 00:27:17.730
Here, we can go back
to Build History.

00:27:17.730 --> 00:27:20.160
And it looks like
the build succeeded.

00:27:20.160 --> 00:27:23.070
We can see that that
first step of deploying

00:27:23.070 --> 00:27:25.140
to App Engine in
this test environment

00:27:25.140 --> 00:27:28.180
and not promoting
it took 17 seconds.

00:27:28.180 --> 00:27:30.010
Spinning up that Ubuntu
image, installing

00:27:30.010 --> 00:27:32.010
a bunch of these
prerequisites, and then running

00:27:32.010 --> 00:27:35.230
our end-to-end integration
tests took another 54 seconds.

00:27:35.230 --> 00:27:38.167
But they did pass,
so that's good.

00:27:38.167 --> 00:27:40.250
And finally, deploying to
production and promoting

00:27:40.250 --> 00:27:43.240
took another 30 seconds.

00:27:43.240 --> 00:27:46.770
Let's go ahead and check out
our application on production.

00:27:46.770 --> 00:27:50.370
We can refresh this page,
and we see that it's now

00:27:50.370 --> 00:27:54.320
updated to Next 2019 Guestbook.

00:27:54.320 --> 00:27:57.260
Now that we've seen how that
CI/CD automated workflow

00:27:57.260 --> 00:27:59.930
works, let's take a look at
that other part of the demo

00:27:59.930 --> 00:28:01.910
that Nikhil talked
about, which is

00:28:01.910 --> 00:28:04.040
posting the messages
to Slack automatically

00:28:04.040 --> 00:28:07.030
to inform your team.

00:28:07.030 --> 00:28:09.450
So here, I have a Cloud
Function already created,

00:28:09.450 --> 00:28:12.710
called CSR to Slack.

00:28:12.710 --> 00:28:15.980
It's watching this Pub/Sub
topic I've called CSR.

00:28:15.980 --> 00:28:18.800
And I've told Cloud
Source Repositories

00:28:18.800 --> 00:28:22.190
to post Pub/Sub events
to that CSR topic

00:28:22.190 --> 00:28:26.643
whenever something happens,
like a new commit coming in.

00:28:26.643 --> 00:28:28.810
I'm then pulling source
code for this Cloud Function

00:28:28.810 --> 00:28:30.930
from a different Cloud
Source Repository

00:28:30.930 --> 00:28:35.050
and executing this
message Slack function.

00:28:35.050 --> 00:28:38.140
Here, we can again go to
Cloud Source Repositories

00:28:38.140 --> 00:28:41.390
and take a look at
what that looks like.

00:28:41.390 --> 00:28:43.240
Finds it right away for me.

00:28:43.240 --> 00:28:46.870
And we can see that
in this function,

00:28:46.870 --> 00:28:50.440
I'm going to be messaging
the Next 2019 channel saying

00:28:50.440 --> 00:28:55.280
that user's email just pushed a
new commit to this repository.

00:28:55.280 --> 00:28:58.030
So let's go over
to Slack, and we

00:28:58.030 --> 00:29:02.325
can see that it indeed did post
that message to that channel.

00:29:02.325 --> 00:29:03.700
Whether you're
trying to interact

00:29:03.700 --> 00:29:06.460
with Slack or any other
third-party service,

00:29:06.460 --> 00:29:08.920
integrations with Cloud
Pub/Sub and Cloud Functions

00:29:08.920 --> 00:29:09.790
make it really easy.

00:29:12.602 --> 00:29:14.060
Now that we've
looked at this demo,

00:29:14.060 --> 00:29:15.960
I'm going to let Nikhil
tell us a little bit more

00:29:15.960 --> 00:29:17.502
about some of the
deploy technologies

00:29:17.502 --> 00:29:19.943
that we have that are
specific to containers.

00:29:19.943 --> 00:29:22.110
So let's go ahead and switch
back to the slides now.

00:29:26.895 --> 00:29:29.130
NIKHIL KAUL: So far,
what Russell's showed

00:29:29.130 --> 00:29:32.700
in the presentation was really
catered towards App Engine.

00:29:32.700 --> 00:29:35.040
But many of you in
the audience might

00:29:35.040 --> 00:29:37.320
be using containers
on a day-to-day basis

00:29:37.320 --> 00:29:39.540
to deploy applications.

00:29:39.540 --> 00:29:41.610
If you're working
with containers,

00:29:41.610 --> 00:29:43.800
as a part of Cloud Build,
we have something called

00:29:43.800 --> 00:29:46.060
as vulnerability scanning.

00:29:46.060 --> 00:29:48.190
Vulnerability
scanning allows you

00:29:48.190 --> 00:29:50.410
to identify security
vulnerabilities

00:29:50.410 --> 00:29:54.110
early on in the software
development process.

00:29:54.110 --> 00:29:57.610
Package vulnerabilities
for Ubuntu, Debian

00:29:57.610 --> 00:30:02.060
are identified right as soon
as the containers are built.

00:30:02.060 --> 00:30:04.550
It also provides you
detailed insight,

00:30:04.550 --> 00:30:08.780
by providing details
such as the severity

00:30:08.780 --> 00:30:12.560
level of the vulnerability,
what is the CVSS score,

00:30:12.560 --> 00:30:15.060
and whether a fix is available.

00:30:15.060 --> 00:30:19.220
In fact, if you want to take
this process to the next level

00:30:19.220 --> 00:30:21.470
and further automate
your tool chain,

00:30:21.470 --> 00:30:23.930
you can use
vulnerability scanning

00:30:23.930 --> 00:30:29.140
with binary authorization, which
will be going [INAUDIBLE] soon.

00:30:29.140 --> 00:30:34.480
Binary authorization essentially
lets you define policies based

00:30:34.480 --> 00:30:37.490
upon your organization needs.

00:30:37.490 --> 00:30:39.920
For instance, as
an organization,

00:30:39.920 --> 00:30:43.750
you can decide
and define that we

00:30:43.750 --> 00:30:47.530
get to deploy only those
containers which are actually

00:30:47.530 --> 00:30:49.120
tested.

00:30:49.120 --> 00:30:53.020
What this means is if some
developer actually ends up

00:30:53.020 --> 00:30:55.210
deploying a container
which was not

00:30:55.210 --> 00:30:58.960
tested as a part of
CI/CD tool chain,

00:30:58.960 --> 00:31:02.030
that container will
not get deployed.

00:31:02.030 --> 00:31:04.960
The [? imageable ?]
here is essentially

00:31:04.960 --> 00:31:07.720
doing something else, however.

00:31:07.720 --> 00:31:09.810
What we're doing over
here is developer

00:31:09.810 --> 00:31:12.640
checks a code into
Cloud Source Repository.

00:31:12.640 --> 00:31:15.400
Cloud Build creates a build.

00:31:15.400 --> 00:31:18.810
The container gets scanned
by vulnerability scanning.

00:31:18.810 --> 00:31:21.450
And then essentially,
binary authorization checks

00:31:21.450 --> 00:31:26.410
for a signature on the image
from vulnerability scanning.

00:31:26.410 --> 00:31:29.050
If a signature is
found, the container

00:31:29.050 --> 00:31:32.280
is deployed to Google
Kubernetes Engine.

00:31:32.280 --> 00:31:34.630
And if a signature is
not found, audit log

00:31:34.630 --> 00:31:41.070
is provided on why the specific
steps were not performed.

00:31:41.070 --> 00:31:43.830
With that, we have
essentially completed

00:31:43.830 --> 00:31:45.780
2/3 of the presentation.

00:31:45.780 --> 00:31:49.020
We specifically looked
at developing an app.

00:31:49.020 --> 00:31:52.200
We specifically also
looked at deploying an app

00:31:52.200 --> 00:31:55.610
by showcasing the Cloud Build
integration with Cloud Source

00:31:55.610 --> 00:31:56.820
Repositories.

00:31:56.820 --> 00:32:00.630
However, as a developer,
many times we also face

00:32:00.630 --> 00:32:03.480
challenges where we
have to debug an app,

00:32:03.480 --> 00:32:05.238
especially in production.

00:32:05.238 --> 00:32:06.780
And we're not able
to replicate those

00:32:06.780 --> 00:32:09.620
issues on a local environment.

00:32:09.620 --> 00:32:12.400
And that's where Cloud
Source Repositories'

00:32:12.400 --> 00:32:16.510
integration with Stackdriver
really comes in handy.

00:32:16.510 --> 00:32:18.700
There are essentially
two ways which

00:32:18.700 --> 00:32:22.120
we allow you to debug
your app in production.

00:32:22.120 --> 00:32:25.080
The first and the
foremost way is

00:32:25.080 --> 00:32:28.940
Stackdriver's was
debug snapshots.

00:32:28.940 --> 00:32:31.120
This comes in really
handy when you're actually

00:32:31.120 --> 00:32:32.870
debugging an app in production.

00:32:32.870 --> 00:32:36.370
Let's take the following
example as an instance.

00:32:36.370 --> 00:32:38.740
You're trying to debug
an app in production,

00:32:38.740 --> 00:32:43.870
but that issue is being faced
only by one specific user.

00:32:43.870 --> 00:32:46.120
And as a result of
which, identifying

00:32:46.120 --> 00:32:48.310
that issue on your
local environment

00:32:48.310 --> 00:32:51.040
becomes really
difficult. And this

00:32:51.040 --> 00:32:56.320
is where Stackdriver's debug
snapshot provides you details.

00:32:56.320 --> 00:32:58.870
It captures the
entire call stacks.

00:32:58.870 --> 00:33:01.220
It inspects the local variables.

00:33:01.220 --> 00:33:03.520
In fact, to do this
you do not even

00:33:03.520 --> 00:33:06.970
have to slow down
or stop your app.

00:33:06.970 --> 00:33:10.510
And the best part is once you
select the lines on which you

00:33:10.510 --> 00:33:13.660
have to set up these
debug snapshots,

00:33:13.660 --> 00:33:16.450
all your running
instances of the app

00:33:16.450 --> 00:33:18.340
can start
automatically capturing

00:33:18.340 --> 00:33:21.140
these debug snapshots.

00:33:21.140 --> 00:33:27.990
The other way of debugging
with Stackdriver is log points.

00:33:27.990 --> 00:33:30.780
RUSSELL WOLF: As Nikhil said,
Stackdriver Debugger also lets

00:33:30.780 --> 00:33:33.170
you set debug log points.

00:33:33.170 --> 00:33:37.520
Debug log points allow you
to set a custom log message

00:33:37.520 --> 00:33:40.070
on a particular line of code.

00:33:40.070 --> 00:33:42.470
That custom log message
can then reference

00:33:42.470 --> 00:33:46.490
the current application
state, as well as only log

00:33:46.490 --> 00:33:48.350
under certain conditions.

00:33:48.350 --> 00:33:52.520
For example, you may want to
log the value of a variable

00:33:52.520 --> 00:33:54.590
only if the value
of that variable

00:33:54.590 --> 00:33:57.560
is outside of an expected range.

00:33:57.560 --> 00:34:01.100
I like to think of it kind
of like printf debugging,

00:34:01.100 --> 00:34:05.430
except you don't need a
single printf in your code.

00:34:05.430 --> 00:34:07.910
And since you're using
Stackdriver Debugger

00:34:07.910 --> 00:34:08.713
in production.

00:34:08.713 --> 00:34:10.130
It's important
that you don't even

00:34:10.130 --> 00:34:15.153
need to restart or
stop your application.

00:34:15.153 --> 00:34:16.570
Finally, Stackdriver
Debugger also

00:34:16.570 --> 00:34:18.670
integrates with
Stackdriver logging,

00:34:18.670 --> 00:34:23.350
which means that you can store,
search, analyze, and alert,

00:34:23.350 --> 00:34:26.070
on these debug events.

00:34:26.070 --> 00:34:29.070
Let's go ahead and take a look
at debug snapshots and debug

00:34:29.070 --> 00:34:32.094
log points in a demo again.

00:34:32.094 --> 00:34:33.719
Can we switch over
to the demo, please?

00:34:41.190 --> 00:34:44.308
Here, we can see the Stackdriver
Debugger user interface.

00:34:47.730 --> 00:34:50.650
At the top, we have
a service selector.

00:34:50.650 --> 00:34:53.400
This allows us to choose
between the various services

00:34:53.400 --> 00:34:56.220
and versions of those services
that we have deployed.

00:34:56.220 --> 00:34:58.220
I'm going to go ahead and
switch over to the one

00:34:58.220 --> 00:35:00.710
that we've now
promoted to 100% and is

00:35:00.710 --> 00:35:02.780
backed by the Cloud
Source Repository

00:35:02.780 --> 00:35:06.030
at the commit we just made.

00:35:06.030 --> 00:35:07.680
Stackdriver Debugger
automatically

00:35:07.680 --> 00:35:10.980
pulls in that source
code and allows

00:35:10.980 --> 00:35:13.800
me to navigate through
the entire file tree.

00:35:13.800 --> 00:35:15.510
We're looking at
guestbook.py now,

00:35:15.510 --> 00:35:18.000
which is where the bulk of
the logic for this application

00:35:18.000 --> 00:35:19.800
exists.

00:35:19.800 --> 00:35:22.890
Now when a user goes
in and posts a message

00:35:22.890 --> 00:35:28.665
to the application, that happens
within this function down here,

00:35:28.665 --> 00:35:29.165
called post.

00:35:31.840 --> 00:35:33.910
Now, I'm sure you've all
been in situations where

00:35:33.910 --> 00:35:37.090
you've had a user running
up against your production

00:35:37.090 --> 00:35:38.260
service.

00:35:38.260 --> 00:35:40.180
They're running into a problem.

00:35:40.180 --> 00:35:41.650
You try reproing it locally.

00:35:41.650 --> 00:35:42.820
It doesn't repro.

00:35:42.820 --> 00:35:45.730
You try reproing it on your
production service as well,

00:35:45.730 --> 00:35:48.050
and you just can't repro it.

00:35:48.050 --> 00:35:51.080
Stackdriver Debugger can
come in to help a lot

00:35:51.080 --> 00:35:53.860
with those types of situations.

00:35:53.860 --> 00:35:57.730
Here, I can go ahead and
go into my snapshot panel

00:35:57.730 --> 00:36:01.570
and set a condition
saying, user email

00:36:01.570 --> 00:36:05.980
equals
cloudsourcerepositories2019--

00:36:05.980 --> 00:36:08.970
which is the name of the email
I'm currently logged in with--

00:36:08.970 --> 00:36:11.110
@gmail.com.

00:36:11.110 --> 00:36:13.990
And let's go ahead
and set this snapshot

00:36:13.990 --> 00:36:16.750
on the greeting.put,
which is where we're

00:36:16.750 --> 00:36:19.750
going to insert the greeting
someone signs in a guestbook

00:36:19.750 --> 00:36:22.420
into our data store.

00:36:22.420 --> 00:36:24.400
And now we have a
snapshot waiting there.

00:36:24.400 --> 00:36:26.275
You know, it may be in
the next five minutes.

00:36:26.275 --> 00:36:27.520
It could be in the next day.

00:36:27.520 --> 00:36:29.980
But when a user comes in
with that email address,

00:36:29.980 --> 00:36:32.800
it's going to capture the
exact application state

00:36:32.800 --> 00:36:34.580
that that user was looking at.

00:36:34.580 --> 00:36:37.580
So we can find out what
was happening for them.

00:36:37.580 --> 00:36:40.200
We can also set
debug log points.

00:36:40.200 --> 00:36:43.010
And so in the meantime, while
we're waiting for that user,

00:36:43.010 --> 00:36:46.290
I kind of want to see what's
happening for other users.

00:36:46.290 --> 00:36:49.917
So I can go in and
click on that same line.

00:36:49.917 --> 00:36:51.750
And for now, I'm just
going to say, if true.

00:36:51.750 --> 00:36:54.800
So I'm always going to have
this debug log happen every time

00:36:54.800 --> 00:36:56.510
someone posts a message.

00:36:56.510 --> 00:37:02.015
And I'm just going to say,
email and log the user email.

00:37:06.285 --> 00:37:07.910
I can then choose to
add that debug log

00:37:07.910 --> 00:37:12.163
point as informational, as
a warning, or as an error.

00:37:12.163 --> 00:37:13.580
And as I said
earlier, all of that

00:37:13.580 --> 00:37:15.410
integrates back with
all of your other

00:37:15.410 --> 00:37:18.632
logs in the Stackdriver logging.

00:37:18.632 --> 00:37:19.840
So I'm going to add that log.

00:37:19.840 --> 00:37:23.752
And now, we just wait
for something to happen.

00:37:23.752 --> 00:37:25.210
So let's go over
to the application

00:37:25.210 --> 00:37:27.230
and make something happen.

00:37:27.230 --> 00:37:30.430
So I'm going to sign the
guestbook again saying,

00:37:30.430 --> 00:37:33.760
thank you all for
coming to our talk.

00:37:36.600 --> 00:37:37.950
And the guestbook is signed.

00:37:37.950 --> 00:37:39.600
And we can return
to Cloud Debugger.

00:37:45.620 --> 00:37:48.273
Here, we can return
to the snapshot.

00:37:48.273 --> 00:37:50.190
And we can see that the
snapshot was captured.

00:37:52.890 --> 00:37:55.830
This user posted in
the default guestbook,

00:37:55.830 --> 00:37:57.060
and that looks right.

00:37:57.060 --> 00:37:59.670
That's the one I
currently have selected.

00:37:59.670 --> 00:38:03.600
And it matched the user
email that I was specifically

00:38:03.600 --> 00:38:04.320
looking for.

00:38:04.320 --> 00:38:06.630
I can then dig into the
other local variables,

00:38:06.630 --> 00:38:08.430
such as inside greeting.

00:38:08.430 --> 00:38:11.010
I can see the value of the
message that was written,

00:38:11.010 --> 00:38:13.260
such as thank you all
for coming to the talk,

00:38:13.260 --> 00:38:15.720
as well as seeing the entire
call stack of the application

00:38:15.720 --> 00:38:18.360
at that point to see if maybe
something was executed that I

00:38:18.360 --> 00:38:22.110
didn't expect to be executed.

00:38:22.110 --> 00:38:24.470
I can also go in and
look at our logs.

00:38:24.470 --> 00:38:28.430
And by refreshing the logs, I
can see that, indeed, this log

00:38:28.430 --> 00:38:29.600
point was hit.

00:38:29.600 --> 00:38:32.900
And we now have a log
in our logging system

00:38:32.900 --> 00:38:34.550
saying this user visited.

00:38:34.550 --> 00:38:39.030
And we get that, if any
other user visited as well.

00:38:39.030 --> 00:38:42.350
So I hope you all enjoyed
seeing Stackdriver Debugger.

00:38:42.350 --> 00:38:45.410
It's actually one of my
favorite tools we have on GCP.

00:38:45.410 --> 00:38:47.612
And I highly suggest
you give it a try.

00:38:47.612 --> 00:38:49.070
I'm going to hand
it back to Nikhil

00:38:49.070 --> 00:38:51.980
now to wrap things up and
explain the main takeaways

00:38:51.980 --> 00:38:52.865
from our talk.

00:38:52.865 --> 00:38:53.320
NIKHIL KAUL: Thanks, Russell.

00:38:53.320 --> 00:38:55.653
RUSSELL WOLF: Will you please
switch back to the slides?

00:38:57.420 --> 00:39:01.040
NIKHIL KAUL: So today, we
saw three specific things.

00:39:01.040 --> 00:39:04.640
We spoke about developing in
applications using Cloud Source

00:39:04.640 --> 00:39:06.140
Repositories.

00:39:06.140 --> 00:39:07.760
During that stage,
we specifically

00:39:07.760 --> 00:39:11.690
saw mirroring source code
from GitHub and Bitbucket

00:39:11.690 --> 00:39:13.850
to Cloud Source Repositories.

00:39:13.850 --> 00:39:17.220
We spoke about how GitLab
integration is coming soon.

00:39:17.220 --> 00:39:21.170
As a part of that process, we
also spoke about code search

00:39:21.170 --> 00:39:23.330
and how code search
lets you search

00:39:23.330 --> 00:39:27.050
across all the repositories
you have access to.

00:39:27.050 --> 00:39:29.480
We showcased Pushblock
functionality,

00:39:29.480 --> 00:39:34.100
which ensures that security keys
are not stored within source

00:39:34.100 --> 00:39:35.990
control management systems.

00:39:35.990 --> 00:39:38.120
That completed the first part.

00:39:38.120 --> 00:39:40.370
During the second
piece, we specifically

00:39:40.370 --> 00:39:43.430
spoke about Cloud Source
Repositories' integration

00:39:43.430 --> 00:39:48.090
with Cloud Build, which lets
you automate your build, test,

00:39:48.090 --> 00:39:50.810
and deployment process
of your pipeline.

00:39:50.810 --> 00:39:53.150
In case you're working
with containers,

00:39:53.150 --> 00:39:55.610
we spoke about how you can
leverage container registry

00:39:55.610 --> 00:39:59.660
vulnerability scanning along
with binary authorization

00:39:59.660 --> 00:40:02.160
to set up specific policies.

00:40:02.160 --> 00:40:04.880
And finally, in the third
part of the presentation,

00:40:04.880 --> 00:40:08.270
we spoke about how you
can use Stackdriver

00:40:08.270 --> 00:40:11.400
to debug applications
in production.

00:40:11.400 --> 00:40:14.230
With that, thank you
